<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Viet Cong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1960">1960</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as National Liberation Front for South Vietnam, National Front for the Liberation of South Vietnam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Viet Cong, formally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> for South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, was the communist-led political and military organisation founded in 1960 to overthrow the government of the Republic of Vietnam. Backed by North Vietnam, it waged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> and launched the Tet Offensive in 1968. In 1969 it formed the Provisional Revolutionary Government, which nominally ruled the south after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> fell in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viet-cong.jpg" alt="Viet Cong" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_National_Liberation_Front_of_South_Vietnam,_Flag_of_South_Vietnam_(1975%E2%80%931976).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Viet Cong is a contraction of Việt Nam cộng sản, meaning Vietnamese communist, a term that acquired a derogatory sense by the late 1940s. After the Geneva Accords of 1954 about 90,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> were evacuated to the North, while 5,000 to 10,000 cadres stayed in the South. Ngo Dinh Diem announced in July 1955 that the election on reunification would not be held, and within a few months his campaign had driven the communists into remote swamps. An assassination campaign began in April 1957.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-vietnam">Communist Party of Vietnam</a> approved a people's war on the South in January 1959, and Group 559 was set up in May to maintain the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> trail. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> was created on 20 December 1960 at Tân Lập village in Tây Ninh Province as a united front meant to draw in non-communists. Many core members were southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> regroupees trained in the North.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The movement grew quickly: an estimated 300,000 members were enrolled in affiliated associations by early 1962, and its first victory over South Vietnamese forces came at Ấp Bắc in January 1963. It used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> and regular units together with a network of cadres. It also used terror as a standard tactic, and death squads killed at least 37,000 civilians, according to the data available, which mostly cover 1967 to 1972.</p><p>In January and February 1968 some 80,000 fighters struck more than 100 towns in the Tet Offensive, including a raid on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> and a massacre of about 3,500 residents at Huế. The hoped-for uprising did not come, and the offensive cost the movement heavily; Trần Văn Trà said about 75,000 were killed or wounded. In June 1969 the front and an urban alliance were merged into the Provisional Revolutionary Government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> on 30 April 1975 the Provisional Revolutionary Government moved into government offices, but authority over the South was given to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-army-of-vietnam">People's Army of Vietnam</a>. People tainted by association with the old government were sent to re-education camps despite protests from non-communist members. The front and the alliance were later merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnamese-fatherland-front">Vietnam Fatherland Front</a>.</p><p>Its character was disputed. Opponents of American involvement said it was an insurgency native to the South, with chairman Nguyễn Hữu Thọ not a communist. Anti-communists said it was a front for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, and Trà's memoirs record that he followed orders from the party's military commission in Hanoi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Viet Cong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1960 the North made war on the Republic of Vietnam through the <b>Viet Cong</b>, sending troops and supplies down the Ho Chi Minh trail, and in 1962 it intensified the infiltration of men and materiel, while Beijing supplied the Viet Cong with small arms and heavier weapons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Border incursions by South Vietnamese troops pursuing <b>Viet Cong</b> insurgents, and overflights by South Vietnamese and United States aircraft, soured relations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">Kingdom of Cambodia (1953-1970) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1968 the population was estimated at 16,259,334, though about one-fifth of the people of the Mekong–Dong Nai delta lived in areas controlled by the <b>Viet Cong</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-vietnam">Republic of Vietnam · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Vietnam War some <b>Viet Cong</b> officers studied it extensively and reportedly could recite entire passages from memory, and General Võ Nguyên Giáp, an avid student of Sun Tzu's ideas, is said to have implemented tactics described in it at the Battle of Dien Bien Phu.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-art-of-war">The Art of War · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>…of Specialist 5 James Gabriel Jr., a Special Forces soldier and the first native Hawaiian to die in Vietnam, killed by <b>Viet Cong</b> fire on a training mission with the South Vietnamese Army on 8 April 1962; a verse that named him was left out of the recording.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-ballad-of-the-green-berets">The Ballad of the Green Berets</a></li><li><blockquote>North Vietnamese and <b>Viet Cong</b> forces attacked towns and bases across South Vietnam during the lunar new year truce.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-30">30 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
